Phantom-WG Modern

WireGuard VPN server as a container stack. phantom-daemon runs
userspace WireGuard, the nftables firewall, multihop exits and the
address pool, with its state in SQLite; auth-service is the
authenticated gateway in front of it; nginx serves the dashboard over
TLS. ./tools/prod.sh installs, runs and updates the stack.


v2.0.0 - 2026-11-14
-------------------

  Container release. The stack runs images that CI publishes to the
  registry and the release pins by digest; ./tools/prod.sh is a
  Python toolchain; update follows a git clone and waits for the
  services; the stack can be served through Cloudflare. An install of
  1.1.5 moves over with MIGRATION. The databases of the daemon and of
  auth-service keep their schemas.

  Moving from 1.1.5:

  * Install. 2.0.0 is installed next to 1.1.5, in a directory of its
    own. The data and the environment files of 1.1.5 are copied into
    it, and the directory of 1.1.5 stays as the way back. MIGRATION
    holds the steps, the checks and the rollback.

  * Host. Docker Engine 27.0.1, Docker Compose 2.27.2, kernel 5.15,
    /dev/net/tun and python3 3.9, each or newer; 1.1.5 asked for
    Docker Engine 20.10 and Compose v2. ./tools/prod.sh gates reports
    what the host has. The versions of Docker and Compose are
    reported, not compared.

  * Environment. .env is new: the image source and the two subnets
    of the stack, with the defaults 1.1.5 fixed in
    docker-compose.yml. .env.daemon is unchanged. In
    .env.auth-service AUTH_PROXY_MAX_BODY drops from 67108864 to
    65536, AUTH_PROXY_BACKUP_MAX_BODY is new with 67108864, and
    AUTH_TRUSTED_PROXIES adds to the subnets of the stack where it
    replaced them.

  * Compose. The published ports move to
    docker-compose.override.yml, which belongs to the host and which
    an update leaves as it is. compose lock and update --skip-compose
    are removed.

  * Installer. get.phantom.tc is retired. 2.0.0 is installed from a
    git clone of main or from a release zip.

  * History. The main branch starts at the release commit of v1.0.0.
    An update of 1.1.5 does not cross it: git stops and the install
    stays as it is.

  Images:

  * Base images. phantom-daemon-base, phantom-auth-base and
    phantom-nginx-base, built by CI from the Dockerfiles of the
    release on debian:trixie-slim, for amd64 and arm64. They hold
    the platform, the Python dependencies and the two bridges; compose
    mounts the code of the daemon and of auth-service and the
    dashboard build read only. 1.1.5 built the daemon and
    auth-service images on the host and ran nginx:1-alpine.

  * Versions. The images carry a version of their own, 1.1.0 in this
    release. tools/compose/registry.yml pins each image by version
    and digest, and tools/compose/MANIFEST.json names the images and
    the Debian source files of their packages.

  * Sources. PHANTOM_IMAGE_SOURCE in .env selects where the images
    come from: registry pulls the pinned images and is the default,
    build builds the same Dockerfiles on the host, archive loads the
    images a release zip carries.

  * Release zip. One zip per architecture, amd64 and arm64, with the
    images in images/ and their SHA256SUMS. It installs without the
    registry. A zip install keeps its version: update follows a git
    clone.

  * Bridges. The daemon image takes firewall-bridge and
    wireguard-go-bridge from their tagged releases, each archive
    checked against the SHA-256 written in the Dockerfile. The
    package carries no vendor tree.

  * nginx. Debian's nginx with nftables, in an image of the stack.

  Image Packages:

    The four packages of 1.1.0, published by
    .github/workflows/release-base-images.yml of the dev/daemon
    branch:
      https://github.com/ARAS-Workspace/phantom-wg/actions/runs/42940486458

    phantom-daemon-base:1.1.0
      https://github.com/ARAS-Workspace/phantom-wg/pkgs/container/phantom-daemon-base

    phantom-auth-base:1.1.0
      https://github.com/ARAS-Workspace/phantom-wg/pkgs/container/phantom-auth-base

    phantom-nginx-base:1.1.0
      https://github.com/ARAS-Workspace/phantom-wg/pkgs/container/phantom-nginx-base

    phantom-sources:1.1.0
      The Debian source files of what the three images install
      (THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES, SOURCE CODE OF THE IMAGES).
      https://github.com/ARAS-Workspace/phantom-wg/pkgs/container/phantom-sources

  Tools:

  * Toolchain. ./tools/prod.sh starts a Python toolchain: the
    standard library only, python3 3.9 or newer on the host, no
    virtual environment. Every command runs in the install directory,
    whatever the working directory is, and a command that fails ends
    with its exit code.

  * gates. Reports Python, Docker, Compose, the kernel, /dev/net/tun,
    git and the free disk. setup and up stop on a kernel under 5.15,
    without /dev/net/tun, or when Docker or Compose does not answer.

  * setup. Creates what is missing and refuses a complete install:
    the environment files and the override from their examples, the
    WireGuard keys, the auth-service keys with the first account and
    its database, the TLS certificate, the address pool.
    --admin-username names the first account, and
    --terazi-ipv4-subnet is checked before the pool is written.

  * Rotation. gen-keys -f, setup-auth -f and setup-tls -f run with
    the stack down. setup-auth -f moves auth.db aside as
    auth.db.<time>.bak.

  * up. Stops on an incomplete setup, and waits until every service
    has run ten seconds without a restart.

  * images. images shows the source and its images, images use
    <source> selects one, and images sync pulls, builds or loads
    what the source needs; pull, build and rebuild each do one.

  * update. Follows a git clone of main: it fetches, moves the clone
    forward, and starts again as the code of the new release, which
    syncs the images, runs its update hook, checks the install,
    recreates the services and waits for them. On local changes, on
    another branch and on a clone that left main it stops with the
    commands that bring the clone back. Nothing is rolled back.

  * certbot. certbot <domain> takes a Let's Encrypt certificate from
    a container built on the host; the same command renews it.

  * add-superadmin. Adds a superadmin through the running
    auth-service; the password is asked on the terminal.

  * hard-reset. Needs the stack down. The data the containers wrote
    as root is removed from a throwaway container, so a user of the
    docker group runs it without sudo. The environment files, the
    override and the Cloudflare mode stay.

  * Owner. The environment files, the keys, the auth database, the
    self-signed certificate and the Cloudflare mode belong to the
    owner of the install directory when root runs the tools, and
    otherwise to the user who runs them. The address pool, the data
    of the running stack and what certbot writes belong to root.

  * help. help lists the commands; help <command> and <command>
    --help print the usage.

  Cloudflare mode:

  * Commands. cloudflare on, refresh, off and status, set from
    .env.cf: an API token of the zone, the zone, and the hostnames
    of the certificate. The zone needs SSL/TLS Full (strict) and
    Authenticated Origin Pulls (Global); on stops on any other
    setting.

  * on. Makes a key on the host, takes an Origin CA certificate for
    it, and writes the mode into docker-compose.override.yml once
    Compose accepts the new file; the last one stays as
    docker-compose.override.yml.bak. A step that fails after the
    certificate revokes it again, until the mode is recorded.

  * nginx. Requires Cloudflare's origin pull certificate, drops on
    port 443 what does not come from Cloudflare's addresses, and
    reads the visitor's address from CF-Connecting-IP: the access
    log and the audit log of auth-service hold the visitor.

  * refresh and off. refresh takes Cloudflare's address list and
    origin pull CA again. off revokes the certificate and puts the
    override back; --keep-certificate takes the mode back without
    the token and leaves the certificate in Cloudflare.

  * Token. The tool holds .env.cf at 600 and hands the token to its
    container on stdin: it is in no argument, no environment and no
    output.

  Stack:

  * Override. docker-compose.override.yml holds the published ports,
    51820/udp and 443. setup creates it from its example, and git
    does not track it.

  * .env. PHANTOM_IMAGE_SOURCE, PHANTOM_SUBNET_V4 and
    PHANTOM_SUBNET_V6; the subnets default to 172.28.0.0/24 and
    fd00:d0ce::/64.

  * Logs. Compose rotates the log of every container: json-file,
    three files of 10 MB.

  * nginx. Listens on IPv4 and IPv6. A request body is 64 KiB at
    most, 64 MiB on the two backup routes. nginx.conf includes mode
    snippets at the http and the server level, present when an
    override mounts them.

  Daemon:

  * Multihop exits. multihop/update replaces the configuration of an
    exit that is not active, and multihop/config returns a stored
    configuration as .conf text.

  * Exit configuration. The parser takes one [Interface] and one
    [Peer] and refuses anything else with INVALID_EXIT_CONFIG and
    the line, the field or the section.

  * Error codes. The exit store answers with its own codes:
    EXIT_NOT_FOUND (404), EXIT_ALREADY_EXISTS (400) and
    EXIT_IS_ACTIVE (400).

  * Address pool. setup creates wallet.db and the daemon opens it;
    up stops when it is missing.

  Dashboard:

  * Backup. A page and a menu entry for the superadmin: export and
    import of the data set.

  * Roles. An admin sees the controls its role holds: the changes of
    the address pool, of DNS and of the multihop exits are for the
    superadmin.

  * Audit. The action filter lists the actions of auth-service
    1.3.0 and the two refused login actions in the rows of 1.1.5,
    the pages are read in the snapshot of the first page, and the
    time column shows its order.

  * WireGuard peers. A table ordered by name, with a filter by name,
    sortable columns for the name, the latest handshake and the two
    byte counts, and pages of 10 to 100.

  * Dashboard. A table of the peers, the latest handshake first. Its
    tiles and the table share one read of the daemon.

  * Multihop. An exit that is not active is edited from its menu:
    the modal opens with the stored configuration and validates it
    with the rules of the daemon before it is sent.

  * Users. A superadmin deletes another superadmin, the own account
    aside.

  * Removed. The dino game of the dashboard and of the not found
    page.

  auth-service 1.1.7 to 1.3.0:

    Seven releases. The proxy forwards a fixed table of routes, the
    roles admin and superadmin are apart, and the audit log names
    who acted in every row. The schema is unchanged.

  * Route table. The proxy forwards the 28 daemon routes of its
    table, matched on method and path. A request outside the table
    is answered 404 and audited without reaching the daemon.

  * Roles. An admin holds 17 of the 28 routes: the reads, the
    multihop status and the client tunnels. The address pool, DNS,
    the multihop exits and backup are for the superadmin; a refusal
    is 403 SUPERADMIN_REQUIRED, before the body is read.

  * Body limits. 64 KiB for a request, 64 MiB on the two backup
    routes: AUTH_PROXY_MAX_BODY and AUTH_PROXY_BACKUP_MAX_BODY.

  * Trusted proxies. The subnets of the stack, read from
    PHANTOM_SUBNET_V4 and PHANTOM_SUBNET_V6; AUTH_TRUSTED_PROXIES
    adds to them.

  * Audit. A row of an account names its actor as "<id>-<name>", a
    row written from the host or by the bootstrap as
    "system:<source>". A route that changes state or hands out a key
    writes every outcome, and a read writes a refusal only. A
    refused /auth request of a signed in account is written when it
    changes state; a refused read is written when the answer is 403.
    GET /auth/audit pages within the snapshot of its first page.

  * Refused logins. A wrong name or password, or too many attempts,
    is one line of the service log, with the client address and the
    name sent, and no audit row.

  * Host superadmin. tools/add_superadmin.py adds a superadmin from
    the host; ./tools/prod.sh add-superadmin runs it.

  * Bootstrap. The keys stay in their files: the output shows
    [REDACTED] in their place.

  * Platform. The image builds on debian:trixie-slim, and Python
    moves from 3.12 to Debian trixie's 3.13.

    Every release in full:
      https://github.com/ARAS-Workspace/phantom-wg/blob/dev/auth-service/CHANGELOG

  Bridges:

    The daemon moves both bridges to v2.2.0.

  * firewall-bridge 2.1.8 to 2.2.0. nft runs as a child process in
    place of the libnftables link, and the bridge writes its table
    to the kernel in one nft transaction.
      https://github.com/ARAS-Workspace/phantom-wg/blob/dev/firewall-bridge/CHANGELOG

  * wireguard-go-bridge 2.1.4 to 2.2.0. A failed call records its
    code and its reason, which the exceptions carry; a device holds
    a lock across a configuration change and the persist of its
    state; the build moves to Go 1.27.3 and Debian trixie.
      https://github.com/ARAS-Workspace/phantom-wg/blob/dev/wireguard-go-bridge/CHANGELOG

  Documentation:

  * SETUP. The prerequisites, the layout of the tools, the commands,
    the logs, the Cloudflare mode, and Setup Recipe: the install as
    commands to run by hand.

  * UPDATE. The update and what an update halfway leaves, Update
    Recipe, the rollback to a tag and the hard reset.

  * MIGRATION. From 1.1.5 to 2.0.0.

  * CHANGELOG. This file, in the package.

  * THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES. The images of the trixie platform,
    nftables in the nginx image, and the source code of the images.

  * SEMVER.md, on the dev/daemon branch. The version of the images
    next to the product version.

